# ENERGY SYSTEMS

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Energy Systems model describes how the human body produces energy to sustain movement.

Every physical action requires energy.

Different actions rely on different energy systems.

The Combat Athlete System uses this model to prescribe training that develops the appropriate physiological demands of combat sports.

---

# Philosophy

Energy systems do not operate independently.

Every movement involves all three systems.

Only their relative contribution changes.

The objective of training is not to isolate an energy system,

but to optimize its contribution to combat performance.

---

# Biological Foundations

The human body continuously regenerates ATP.

CAS recognizes three primary energy systems.

ATP-PC System

Anaerobic Glycolytic System

Oxidative System

Each system contributes simultaneously.

---

# ATP-PC System

Primary Characteristics

Immediate energy production.

Highest power output.

Very limited capacity.

Primary Fuel

Stored ATP.

Phosphocreatine.

Typical Duration

Approximately 0–10 seconds.

Examples

Maximum punch.

Maximum kick.

Explosive takedown.

Vertical jump.

Heavy lift.

Short sprint.

Combat Importance

Critical for explosive actions.

Primary Limitation

Rapid depletion.

Requires recovery before maximal output can be repeated.

---

# Anaerobic Glycolytic System

Primary Characteristics

High power.

Moderate capacity.

Produces energy rapidly.

Primary Fuel

Muscle glycogen.

Blood glucose.

Typical Duration

Approximately 10–90 seconds.

Examples

Extended striking exchanges.

Scrambles.

Repeated takedown attempts.

High-intensity circuits.

Combat Importance

Supports repeated explosive efforts.

Primary Limitation

Metabolic fatigue.

Accumulation of hydrogen ions.

---

# Oxidative System

Primary Characteristics

Lowest power.

Largest capacity.

Primary Fuels

Carbohydrates.

Fat.

To a lesser extent,

protein.

Typical Duration

Several minutes to hours.

Examples

Recovery between exchanges.

Recovery between rounds.

Long technical sessions.

General conditioning.

Combat Importance

Supports recovery and repeated performance.

---

# Interaction Between Systems

Energy systems never function independently.

Examples

Explosive punch

ATP-PC dominant

↓

Recovery between combinations

Oxidative contribution

↓

Extended exchange

Increasing glycolytic contribution

The dominant system changes continuously.

---

# Energy Demands of Combat Sports

Different sports emphasize different energy profiles.

Examples

Boxing

High ATP-PC demand.

High glycolytic demand.

Strong oxidative support.

Muay Thai

High ATP-PC demand.

High glycolytic demand.

Moderate oxidative contribution.

Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu

Moderate ATP-PC demand.

Moderate glycolytic demand.

Very high oxidative contribution.

Wrestling

Very high ATP-PC demand.

Very high glycolytic demand.

High oxidative contribution.

Krav Maga

Balanced contribution from all systems.

Training should reflect sport-specific demands.

---

# Repeated High-Intensity Efforts

Combat rarely involves a single maximal effort.

Athletes must repeatedly regenerate ATP.

Recovery capacity therefore becomes a major performance factor.

---

# Energy System Development

ATP-PC Development

Examples

Heavy lifting.

Olympic lifting.

Plyometrics.

Short maximal sprints.

Medicine ball throws.

Objective

Increase explosive capacity.

---

# Glycolytic Development

Examples

Heavy bag intervals.

Repeated sprint training.

Combat circuits.

Specific sparring intervals.

Objective

Improve tolerance to repeated high-intensity efforts.

---

# Oxidative Development

Examples

Zone 2 conditioning.

Tempo intervals.

Aerobic circuits.

Long technical sessions.

Objective

Improve recovery capacity.

---

# Energy Economy

Efficient athletes consume less energy for the same movement.

Examples

Relaxed striking.

Efficient footwork.

Technical precision.

Reduced unnecessary muscular tension.

Movement economy improves performance without increasing physiological capacity.

---

# Fatigue

Different systems produce different fatigue profiles.

ATP-PC

Neuromuscular fatigue.

Glycolytic

Metabolic fatigue.

Oxidative

Cardiovascular fatigue.

Programming should consider these differences.

---

# Recovery

Recovery depends upon:

Sleep.

Nutrition.

Hydration.

Aerobic fitness.

Training history.

Stress.

Recovery capacity directly influences repeated performance.

---

# Assessment

Possible assessments include:

Wingate Test.

Repeated Sprint Ability Test.

Yo-Yo Intermittent Recovery Test.

Heart Rate Recovery.

Blood Lactate Testing.

VO₂max.

Sport-specific conditioning tests.

Assessment should remain relevant to combat performance.

